When SAP opens the Windows “Save As” dialog, normal SAP GUI scripting cannot control it because it is a Windows OS dialog, not part of SAP. To automate entering the file path and filename, you usually use VBA + SendKeys or Windows Script Host.

Below are common methods.


---

Method 1 — Use VBA SendKeys (Simplest)

After you click Spreadsheet, SAP opens the Windows Save As window.
You can send keystrokes to fill the filename and press Save.

Example VBA Macro

Sub SAP_SaveAs_Automation()

    Dim SapGuiAuto As Object
    Dim SAPApp As Object
    Dim SAPCon As Object
    Dim session As Object
    
    Dim filePath As String
    
    filePath = "C:\SAPExports\RoleAssignment.xlsx"

    Set SapGuiAuto = GetObject("SAPGUI")
    Set SAPApp = SapGuiAuto.GetScriptingEngine
    Set SAPCon = SAPApp.Children(0)
    Set session = SAPCon.Children(0)

    'Export spreadsheet
    session.findById("wnd[0]/usr/cntlGRID1/shellcont/shell").pressToolbarContextButton "&MB_EXPORT"
    session.findById("wnd[0]/usr/cntlGRID1/shellcont/shell").selectContextMenuItem "&XXL"

    'Wait for Save As dialog
    Application.Wait Now + TimeValue("0:00:02")

    'Type path and filename
    SendKeys filePath, True

    'Press Save
    SendKeys "{ENTER}", True

End Sub

How it works

1. SAP opens Save As


2. Macro waits 2 seconds


3. SendKeys types full file path


4. {ENTER} presses Save




---

Method 2 — Use WScript.Shell (More Reliable)

This is better than plain SendKeys.

Sub SAP_SaveAs_Automation()

    Dim shell As Object
    Set shell = CreateObject("WScript.Shell")

    'Wait for Save As dialog
    Application.Wait Now + TimeValue("0:00:02")

    shell.SendKeys "C:\SAPExports\RoleAssignment.xlsx"
    shell.SendKeys "{ENTER}"

End Sub


---

Method 3 — Use SAP Built-in Export (BEST if available)

Sometimes SAP still shows its own dialog first, which is scriptable.

Example:

session.findById("wnd[1]/usr/ctxtDY_PATH").Text = "C:\SAPExports"
session.findById("wnd[1]/usr/ctxtDY_FILENAME").Text = "RoleAssignment.xlsx"
session.findById("wnd[1]/tbar[0]/btn[11]").press

If this dialog appears, you do NOT need SendKeys.


---

Important Tips

1️⃣ Always add a delay

Windows dialog takes time to open.

Application.Wait Now + TimeValue("0:00:02")


---

2️⃣ Use full path

Example:

C:\SAPExports\Roles_20260313.xlsx


---

3️⃣ Prevent overwriting

Add timestamp.

filePath = "C:\SAPExports\Roles_" & Format(Now, "yyyymmdd_hhmmss") & ".xlsx"


---

Best Practice for SAP Automation

Professional SAP automation normally uses:

SAP GUI scripting

Excel VBA

Power Automate

AutoHotkey


because Windows Save As dialogs cannot be fully controlled by SAP scripting alone.


